<figure class="cp-figure" data-keys aria-labelledby="cp-keys-title">
 <figcaption id="cp-keys-title"><span>Figure 4</span> Which keys can the new writes separate?</figcaption>
 <div class="cp-options"><label>Key design <select data-design><option value="edge">Time first</option><option value="customer">Customer first</option><option value="full">Hash the whole history key</option><option value="prefix">Hash customer only</option></select></label></div>
 <div class="cp-scene"><p class="cp-label" data-key-label data-live></p><div data-key-rows data-live></div><div class="cp-read" data-key-read data-live></div></div>
 <p class="cp-explanation" data-key-explanation data-live aria-live="polite"></p>
 <p class="cp-caption">A conceptual key-distribution model. A, B and C stand for successive new orders for common customer 1001; X and Y are symbolic orders for customers 1002 and 1003. Four drawn buckets and the assignments are illustrative, not native hash values, four guaranteed ranges or four nodes. Each bar shows key order within a prefix.</p>
 <details><summary>Designs as text</summary><p>Time first sends successive writes towards one edge. Customer first gives different customers separate prefixes but keeps A, B and C together. Hashing the full key can put A, B and C in different buckets, so one customer’s ordered history must be found across buckets and combined. Hashing only customer keeps that history together in one bucket, including its concentrated writes.</p></details>
</figure>
<script>
 document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-keys]').forEach(root=>{
 const select=root.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-design]')!;
 const modes={
 edge:{label:'(placed_at, …) · later times →',rows:[['Older ranges','older keys'],['Newest range','A → X → B → Y → C']],read:'One advancing edge receives the latest writes.',text:'After a split, later timestamps still land beyond the earlier ones. Old data can move away while the active edge stays busy.'},
 customer:{label:'(customer_id, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)',rows:[['Customer 1001','C · B · A'],['Customer 1002','X'],['Customer 1003','Y']],read:'Customer 1001’s ordered history stays together.',text:'Customer prefixes separate different customers’ demand. They do not disperse the dominant customer’s own new orders.'},
 full:{label:'(hash(full key), customer_id, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)',rows:[['Bucket 0','B'],['Bucket 1','A · Y'],['Bucket 2','C'],['Bucket 3','X']],read:'History for 1001: search four buckets → combine C, B, A.',text:'Different order keys for 1001 can land in different buckets. A customer and time condition alone does not identify the full-key hash, so ordered history pays for multiple bucket searches and combination.'},
 prefix:{label:'(hash(customer_id), customer_id, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)',rows:[['Bucket 0','Y'],['Bucket 1','C · B · A'],['Bucket 2','X'],['Bucket 3','—']],read:'History for 1001: one known bucket → C, B, A.',text:'Knowing customer 1001 determines its bucket and preserves its history order. It also keeps all of that customer’s A, B and C writes in the same bucket. This repairs cross-customer distribution, not within-customer concentration.'}
 };
 function draw(){const m=modes[select.value as keyof typeof modes];root.querySelector('[data-key-label]')!.textContent=m.label;const rows=root.querySelector('[data-key-rows]')!;rows.replaceChildren(...m.rows.map(([label,keys])=>{const row=document.createElement('div');row.className='cp-bucket';const name=document.createElement('span');name.textContent=label;const key=document.createElement('strong');key.textContent=keys;row.append(name,key);return row}));root.querySelector('[data-key-read]')!.textContent=m.read;root.querySelector('[data-key-explanation]')!.textContent=m.text}
 select.addEventListener('change',draw);draw();
 });
</script>
